## 16. Deep update — 2026-10-02: maturity reassessment

This section updates the original research after a fresh review of the current TensorFold documentation.

### 16.1 Why the classification changed

The important change is not simply that CUDA now exists. TensorFold now exposes a coherent specialized-runtime stack:

- Apple Silicon / MLX execution;
- NVIDIA / CUDA execution;
- family-specific CUDA engines;
- explicit model and checkpoint recipes;
- one- and two-rank CUDA execution for selected families;
- MTP, DFlash2 and prompt-lookup drafting;
- serial references inside the same engine;
- byte-level and token-level exactness checks;
- memory-aware context admission;
- an OpenAI-compatible API;
- documented CUDA benchmarks on GB10 / DGX Spark;
- detailed engineering notes covering kernels, CUDA graphs, NCCL, prefix caches and page migration.

The official website currently describes TensorFold as a parallel-decoding runtime for Apple Silicon and NVIDIA GPUs with an OpenAI-compatible API. Its public example uses Nemotron 3.5 Lightning and exposes the client base URL at http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1. The site also publishes earlier MLX measurements while explicitly stating that current-release benchmarks are pending. This is much stronger evidence of implementation maturity than the initial prototype-oriented assessment.

### 16.2 Current CUDA capability matrix

The current NVIDIA runbook is operational documentation, not merely a future roadmap. It specifies an NVIDIA PyTorch container, installation inside that environment, CUDA serving commands and family-specific requirements.

| Family | CUDA lane | Draft mechanism | Rank topology |
|---|---|---|---|
| Qwen3.8-27B | documented | DFlash2; serial with no drafts | 1 or 2 |
| Qwen3.8 Flash Next | documented | MTP | 1 or 2 |
| GLM-5.3-Flash | documented | MTP and DFlash2 | 2 |
| Nemotron | documented | included MTP head | 1 or 2 |

This must be interpreted as a capability matrix, not a universal compatibility statement. Each row depends on the exact checkpoint, quantization/layout, context, memory and hardware topology.

### 16.3 Current CUDA benchmark evidence

The CUDA recipe book reports measurements on NVIDIA GB10 / DGX Spark hardware:

| Workload | Reported TensorFold result versus vLLM with MTP |
|---|---:|
| Qwen3.8-27B, 1 Spark | 2.70–3.05x |
| Qwen3.8-27B, 2 Sparks | 1.94–2.49x |
| Qwen3.8 Flash Next, 1 Spark | 1.60–1.79x |
| Qwen3.8 Flash Next, 2 Sparks | 1.74–2.24x |
| GLM-5.3-Flash, 2 Sparks | 1.78–2.06x |

The documented conditions include one stream, 64-token replies, greedy and sampled workloads and medians over multiple seeds. TensorFold's drafted results are checked against its serial reference.

These are project-reported measurements. They are not LEONES measurements and must not be converted into RTX 3050 performance estimates.

### 16.4 Exactness is a scoped engineering contract

TensorFold's exactness claim should be recorded precisely:

same TensorFold engine + same weights + same runtime settings + same sampling rule = speculative result identical to the serial reference.

The CUDA recipe book explains that row-invariant kernels are used so that a row verified inside a multi-row window receives the same bits as its one-row serial execution. This is a same-engine contract.

It does not establish cross-runtime numerical identity. LEONES must not turn “TensorFold speculative equals TensorFold serial” into “TensorFold equals vLLM, llama.cpp or stock PyTorch”.

### 16.5 Family-specific engineering is now a first-class property

TensorFold is better modeled as a set of specialized execution engines behind a common serving interface than as a universal transformer loader.

The GLM recipe is a particularly clear example: the current CUDA implementation uses two 128 GB GB10 systems, a roughly 182 GB 4-bit checkpoint, tensor parallelism, NCCL, CUDA graphs, MTP/DFlash2 drafting and extensive exactness tests.

The DeepSeek-V4-Flash recipe shows the same specialization on MLX: complex family-specific kernels, compressed/routed structures, custom draft/verify logic and long-context memory accounting. That recipe explicitly states that its CUDA path on two DGX Sparks is not yet implemented.

Therefore ODS should record family support independently from runtime support.

### 16.6 Memory admission must be explicit

The current runbook states that model-file size is not the complete process footprint and that CUDA context admission can fail when available memory is insufficient. Requested reply length also needs cache space.

For ODS, a TensorFold candidate should therefore contain at least:

- runtime;
- backend;
- exact family;
- exact checkpoint and revision;
- quantization/layout;
- required GPU class;
- required and observed VRAM;
- required and observed RAM;
- rank count;
- admitted context;
- draft mechanism;
- evidence source and evidence class.

A model must not be marked TensorFold-compatible from its architecture name alone.

### 16.7 RTX 3050 4 GB: current evidence boundary

The current LEONES target remains:

GPU: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3050 Laptop GPU  
VRAM: 4 GB  
System RAM: approximately 14–16 GB available to the ODS environment  
Backend: NVIDIA CUDA

Current evidence establishes:

- TensorFold CUDA exists: OBSERVED.
- Family-specific CUDA engines exist: OBSERVED.
- OpenAI-compatible serving exists: OBSERVED.
- RTX 3050 4 GB compatibility: NOT ESTABLISHED.
- RTX 3050 throughput: NOT MEASURED.
- ODS versus TensorFold comparative benchmark: NOT MEASURED.

This is an evidence boundary, not a negative compatibility verdict.

A 4-bit checkpoint being smaller than 4 GB would still not prove that the whole process fits. Runtime state, KV cache, activations, temporary buffers, CUDA allocations and other overheads must also fit.

### 16.8 Why P1 is justified despite the lack of RTX 3050 validation

P1 describes integration priority, not hardware compatibility.

TensorFold now has:

1. a concrete service boundary;
2. a real CUDA implementation;
3. reproducible family-specific recipes;
4. a model/checkpoint capability matrix;
5. speculative-decoding infrastructure;
6. explicit exactness methodology;
7. multi-rank execution;
8. published CUDA measurements;
9. memory/context admission logic;
10. enough architecture to justify serious ODS integration research.

This combination is materially different from a radar-only project.

### 16.9 Recommended ODS architecture

The clean boundary is:

ODS capability registry
→ model/family admission
→ checkpoint admission
→ hardware/VRAM admission
→ rank/topology admission
→ draft-model admission
→ TensorFold service
→ OpenAI-compatible /v1 API

ODS applications should not need to know whether the selected model is served by llama-server or TensorFold.

A future optional service could use an extensions/services/tensorfold structure with a manifest, Compose definition, Dockerfile, health check and integration documentation. The manifest should declare supported families, checkpoint constraints, memory requirements, rank topology, draft requirements and evidence status.

ODS should not automatically download a large checkpoint merely because TensorFold is enabled.

### 16.10 Correct admission workflow

The LEONES flow remains:

DISCOVERY → PROFILE → CANDIDATES → CHOICE → CONSENT → INSTALL → PHYSICAL VERIFICATION → BENCHMARK → MEASUREMENT → EVIDENCE

For TensorFold specifically:

1. detect the hardware;
2. read the current family/checkpoint support;
3. filter by VRAM, RAM, context and rank requirements;
4. identify the smallest viable checkpoint;
5. obtain explicit download consent;
6. serve it;
7. check health;
8. query /v1/models;
9. run a minimal completion;
10. benchmark;
11. record evidence separately from estimates and project-reported results.

### 16.11 Benchmark fields required by LEONES

A future TensorFold test should record:

- TensorFold version and commit;
- exact model repository and revision;
- checkpoint files and quantization;
- draft model and revision;
- GPU, VRAM, driver and CUDA version;
- CPU and RAM;
- container image;
- Python version;
- launch arguments;
- ranks and NCCL configuration;
- context;
- kernel compilation time;
- model load time;
- TTFT;
- prompt tok/s;
- generation tok/s;
- peak VRAM;
- peak RAM;
- speculative acceptance and commit rate;
- serial versus speculative token IDs or hashes;
- repeated-run stability;
- long-prompt behavior.

If the identical model/checkpoint can run in another ODS runtime, the comparison must use the same hardware, revision, prompts, context, generation settings and repeated-run protocol.

### 16.12 Evidence taxonomy

LEONES should preserve four classes:

- REPORTED — published by TensorFold;
- OBSERVED — verified in source or runbook;
- ESTIMATED — inferred before execution;
- MEASURED — produced by LEONES on the target machine.

Only MEASURED evidence can establish actual RTX 3050 performance.

### 16.13 Documentation maturity caveat

There is an important split in the current public documentation.

The root README still emphasizes the V0/V1 memory-virtualization and MLX foundation. The current RUNBOOK and CUDA recipes contain much more detailed NVIDIA operational guidance and family-specific benchmark procedures.

For LEONES this should be interpreted as:

root README = architectural foundation  
RUNBOOK + family recipes = current operational CUDA evidence

The runbook also explicitly marks release-qualified memory and speed results as pending under its release-0.3.5 note. Therefore the project is mature enough for P1 research, but not every current memory/performance figure should be described as release-qualified final data.

### 16.14 Updated conclusion

TensorFold now deserves P1 placement because it has moved beyond the earlier prototype/radar profile into a documented specialized runtime with concrete CUDA family engines, reproducible recipes, speculative decoding, same-engine exactness and an OpenAI-compatible serving boundary.

The correct ODS model is:

TensorFold = specialized execution provider + family-specific admission + checkpoint-specific admission + hardware-specific admission + evidence-driven validation.

For the RTX 3050 4 GB target, no compatibility or throughput claim should yet be made. The next research action is to identify the smallest currently supported CUDA checkpoint and physically test it before modifying the ODS installation.

**Revised LEONES classification: P1 — high-priority integration research.**
